{
  "index": 8,
  "response": "```python\ndef bubble_sort(a):\n    for i in range(len(a)):\n        for j in range(len(a) - i - 1):\n            if a[j] > a[j + 1]:\n                swap(a, j, j + 1)\n\n\ndef swap(a, i, j):\n    temp = a[i]\n    a[i] = a[j]\n    a[j] = temp\n\n\na = [\"a\", \"c\", \"e\", \"b\", \"d\"]\nbubble_sort(a)\nprint(a)\n```\n\n**Output:**\n```\n[\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"d\", \"e\"]\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n\n1. The `bubble_sort` function implements the bubble sort algorithm.\n2. It uses two nested loops to iterate over the array.\n3. Inside the outer loop, it compares the current element with the next element.\n4. If the current element is greater than the next element, it swaps the two elements.\n5. The `swap` function swaps the elements at positions `i` and `j`.\n6. The algorithm continues until it reaches the end of the array, or it finds a swapped element.\n7. Finally, it returns the sorted array.\n\n**Note:**\n\n* The time complexity of bubble sort is O(n^2), where n is the length of the array.\n* Bubble sort is not an efficient sorting algorithm for large arrays.\n* It is often used as a simple example of a sorting algorithm.",
